<h2>Verified resale tickets: what it means</h2>
<p>Resale tickets are tickets sold by someone other than the venue box office, such as a fan, a season ticket holder or a broker. People often search for verified resale tickets to mean resale tickets that a marketplace stands behind. On Seat Outlet that backing is the guarantee above: orders are fulfilled through the TicketNetwork marketplace, and the guarantee covers authentic and valid tickets, delivery in time for the event, the tickets you ordered or better, and a refund if the event is canceled (delivery fees excluded). Seat Outlet is not the venue or the original seller, and prices are set by sellers.</p>
<h3>Are resale tickets legit and safe?</h3>
<p>Resale tickets can be a safe way to buy when the marketplace has a clear guarantee, a secure checkout and real support. Risk grows when you pay a stranger directly, ask to be paid by transfer apps, or buy from a social media post. Read our guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-avoid-ticket-scams">avoiding ticket scams</a>. Resale rules differ by state and event, so check them if you are unsure.</p>
<h3>Legit ticket sites: what to look for</h3>
<ul>
  <li>A written guarantee that says what is covered and what is not.</li>
  <li>A secure checkout and a clear total before you pay.</li>
  <li>Contact details and a way to reach a person after you buy.</li>
  <li>Prices shown in full, with the seller type explained.</li>
</ul>
<p>See how our <a href="/worry-free-guarantee">worry free guarantee</a> answers each point.</p>

<h2>A checklist before you buy</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Confirm the event name, date, time and venue.</li>
  <li>Check the section, row and number of seats.</li>
  <li>Read the delivery method so you know how tickets will reach you.</li>
  <li>Review the full cost at checkout.</li>
  <li>Save your confirmation email and order number.</li>
</ol>
<p>The Federal Trade Commission has <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/how-avoid-scam" target="_blank" rel="noopener">straightforward advice on how to avoid a scam</a>, and our own guide covers <a href="/blog/how-to-avoid-ticket-scams">ticket scams</a> in detail.</p>

<h2>If something goes wrong</h2>
<p>Act quickly and keep records. Contact <a href="/ticket-customer-service">customer service</a> with your order number and details, ideally well before the event. You can also email <a href="mailto:support@seatoutlet.com">support@seatoutlet.com</a>. We cannot give a fixed number of days for a refund, because timing depends on the payment provider and the event.</p>
<p>Many questions are answered in the <a href="/ticket-faq">ticket FAQ</a>. For the legal wording, see our <a href="/terms-and-conditions">terms and conditions</a>.</p>

